};
```

# Middleware | Guides 📚 Qwik Documentation
created: 2023-02-03, last updated: 2026-07-20, [Read more](https://qwik.dev/docs/middleware/)

All middleware functions are passed a `RequestEvent` object which can be used to control the flow of HTTP response.
For example, you can read/write cookies, headers, redirect, produce responses and exit the middleware chain early.
Middleware functions are executed in the order as described above, from the topmost `layout.tsx` to the last `index.tsx`.
...
### cookie
Use `cookie` to set and retrieve cookie information for a request.
Middleware can manually read and set cookies, using the `cookie` function.
This might be useful for setting a session cookie, such as a JWT token, or a cookie to track a user.
```
import { type RequestHandler } from '@builder.io/qwik-city';
export const onGet: RequestHandler = async ({ cookie, json }) => {
let count = cookie.get('Qwik.demo.count')?.number() || 0;
count++;
cookie.set('Qwik.demo.count', count);
json(200, { count });
};
...
Redirect to a new URL.
Notice the importance of throwing to prevent other middleware functions from running.
The `redirect()` method will automatically set the `Location` header to the redirect URL.
